 3.4  Counterexample search and refutation 

 
 Every candidate conjecture that survives novelty and selection advances to this stage.
Because there are infinitely many non-isomorphic finite graphs and the current snapshot  T T  is only a partial view,
“true on the table” is a statement about present evidence, not a
generalisation claim. Therefore, the system that tries to find robust conjectures must actively search for counterexamples. There are several approaches to automated refutation. For a review see  Jooken (2025) . Recently, deep reinforcement learning  Wagner (2021)  has been applied for counterexample search, building counterexample graphs edge by edge guided by the conjecture’s violation margin. 
 
 
